  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "### Fixed\n\n- **No resource could be assigned in the inspector.** Every `[GlobalClass]` resource was missing\n  `[Tool]`. Without it Godot never instantiates the managed C# object in an editor context: it creates\n  the bare engine `Resource` and hands *that* to the inspector, so assigning one to a typed `[Export]`\n  threw `InvalidCastException: Unable to cast object of type 'Godot.Resource' to type\n  'Noodloft.Components.Addon.Resources.HealthResource'`.\n\n  The nodes were unaffected only because they already carried `[Tool]` for their configuration\n  warnings, which is exactly why the gap stayed invisible: the half of the addon that wanted the\n  attribute for an unrelated reason worked, and the half with no reason to want it did not.\n\n  Nothing about the declarations was wrong — the generator emitted `[ScriptPath]`, a `ResourceType`\n  hint and the right hint string for every one of them. That is why this survived every compile check\n  in the repo and two releases that claimed to fix the symptom: the code described the right object,\n  and the editor never built it. `[Tool]` is now on all fifteen resources, with the reason written\n  beside each so it does not get tidied away as noise.\n\n  `NoodloftSaveManager` is deliberately left without it. It is an autoload, never assigned to an\n  export, and making it run in the editor would start a save session there.\n\n  **0.0.1-alpha6 said any `.tres` saved while the addon was broken had to be recreated. That was\n  wrong** — nothing was ever wrong with those files. The editor could not build the resource in the\n  first place, so there was no bad file to throw away. Keep them.",
